---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { faqPageSchema } from '../schema/faqPage';

const meta = {
  title: 'For Families: How to Help a Loved One With Addiction',
  description:
    'Guidance for spouses, parents, and adult children — how to start the recovery conversation, set boundaries with love, and support healing.',
  primaryKeyword: 'how to help a loved one with addiction',
};

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/design-preview' },
  { name: 'Family Resources', url: '/family-resources' },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What do I say when I first bring up treatment?',
    answer:
      'Choose a calm moment — not the middle of a crisis or a heated argument. Use "I" statements that express concern without blame: "I love you and I\'m scared about what I\'m seeing." Avoid ultimatums unless you mean them and are prepared to follow through. You don\'t have to have all the answers ready. Starting the conversation is the goal.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I set limits without cutting my loved one off?',
    answer:
      'Setting limits means deciding what you will and won\'t accept in your own life — not punishing the person you love. You might stop covering financial consequences of their use, decline to participate in situations where they\'re actively intoxicated, or require that family events remain substance-free. The goal is to stop enabling without abandoning.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a family intervention, and should we try one?',
    answer:
      'An intervention is a structured, planned conversation where family members share how addiction has affected them and ask the person to accept help. It works best when guided by a professional and when family members are unified, calm, and prepared — not as a surprise ambush but as an act of love. Contact our admissions team at (530) 528-2342 for guidance before organizing one.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Sunbright involve families during treatment?',
    answer:
      'With the resident\'s consent, we keep families informed of general progress, facilitate structured family communication, and provide access to our family resource library. Our goal is to help the whole family heal — not just the person in treatment. Family therapy and education sessions are available as part of our program.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What if my loved one refuses to get help?',
    answer:
      'You cannot force recovery, but you can stop making it easier to continue using. Focus on your own health, seek support (Al-Anon and Nar-Anon are free peer resources), and keep the door open. Sometimes people need time and repeated gentle invitations. If your loved one is in immediate danger, call 911.',
  },
];

const schema = faqPageSchema(faqs);
---

<SiteLayout {...meta} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} schema={schema}>
  <header class="page-header">
    <div class="wrap ph-inner">
      <span class="eyebrow">For Families</span>
      <h1>How to help a <em>loved one with addiction</em> — and not lose yourself.</h1>
      <p class="lead">If someone you love is struggling with alcohol or drug use, you're probably exhausted, scared, and not sure what to do next. This page is for you. We'll walk through how to recognize the problem, how to start the conversation, how to set loving limits, and how Sunbright can help your whole family heal.</p>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a href="tel:+15305282342" class="btn btn-gold">Call (530) 528-2342</a>
        <a href="/admissions" class="btn btn-ghost">Admissions Process</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section section--white">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="prose wide">
        <p>Loving someone in addiction is one of the hardest things a family can go through. The worry, the helplessness, the cycles of hope and disappointment — they take a toll that doesn't get talked about enough. You are not alone, and the fact that you're here, looking for guidance, matters.</p>

        <h2>Recognizing when a problem has become addiction</h2>
        <p>Most people who develop a serious relationship with alcohol or drugs didn't plan to. It often starts as a way to manage pain, stress, or a mental health struggle — and gradually becomes something that takes over. Signs that use may have crossed into addiction include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Loss of control.</strong> They drink or use more than they intend to, and can't reliably stop once they start.</li>
          <li><strong>Continued use despite consequences.</strong> Health problems, lost jobs, relationship damage — and they continue anyway.</li>
          <li><strong>Withdrawal or withdrawal anxiety.</strong> Physical discomfort or intense anxiety when they haven't used recently.</li>
          <li><strong>Abandoning things they used to care about.</strong> Hobbies, relationships, responsibilities that have quietly disappeared.</li>
          <li><strong>Denial or minimization.</strong> Insisting it's not a problem, or that they could stop anytime they wanted.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If several of these sound familiar, it may be time to explore treatment. You can <a href="/what-we-treat/alcohol">learn more about alcohol use disorder</a> or <a href="/what-we-treat/opioids">opioid addiction</a> on our treatment pages.</p>

        <h2>How to start the conversation</h2>
        <p>There is no perfect script — but there are approaches that tend to work better than others. The goal of the first conversation is not to get an immediate "yes" to treatment. It is to open a door.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Choose your moment carefully.</strong> Talk when they are sober, calm, and you have privacy and time. Not during a crisis, not in front of others, not when either of you is flooded with emotion.</li>
          <li><strong>Lead with love, not accusation.</strong> "I've been worried about you" lands differently than "you've been destroying everything." Both may be true, but the first one is more likely to keep them in the room.</li>
          <li><strong>Be specific without cataloguing every mistake.</strong> "I noticed you didn't come home Tuesday night, and I couldn't reach you. It scared me" is more effective than a lengthy list of grievances.</li>
          <li><strong>Have information ready, not a demand.</strong> Know the name and number of a treatment center — like ours, <a href="tel:+15305282342">(530) 528-2342</a> — so if they're open in that moment, you can move quickly.</li>
          <li><strong>Expect resistance, and don't take it personally.</strong> Denial is a feature of addiction, not a character flaw. The first conversation rarely results in immediate agreement. That doesn't mean it failed.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Setting limits with love</h2>
        <p>Limits are often called "boundaries," but the word has gotten muddled. What we mean is this: deciding what you will and won't participate in — not as punishment, but as an act of integrity and self-preservation.</p>
        <p>Limits are not ultimatums designed to force change. They are decisions about your own life. Common, healthy limits families set include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Not paying bills or debts caused directly by substance use</li>
          <li>Not allowing substances to be used in the family home</li>
          <li>Not covering for a loved one at work or with extended family</li>
          <li>Not riding in a car with someone who is impaired</li>
          <li>Not attending family gatherings where the person will be actively intoxicated</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Setting and holding limits is genuinely hard. It can feel like abandonment when it is actually the opposite — it is refusing to help the addiction continue. If you need support, Al-Anon (for families of people with alcohol use disorder) and Nar-Anon (for families of people with drug addiction) are free peer support groups that have helped millions of people in your position.</p>

        <h2>What an intervention is — and isn't</h2>
        <p>The word "intervention" has been sensationalized by reality television. In practice, a well-done intervention is simply a structured, planned conversation where family members and close friends share how a person's addiction has affected them — calmly, specifically, and with love — and ask them to accept help that is ready to go.</p>
        <p>The key elements of an intervention that actually works:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Professional guidance.</strong> An interventionist or clinical advisor can help you prepare, coach everyone involved, and manage the conversation in real time.</li>
          <li><strong>A unified, prepared group.</strong> Everyone should be on the same page, with pre-written statements, and agree on what they will and won't accept going forward.</li>
          <li><strong>Treatment ready to go.</strong> If your loved one says yes, you want to be able to act on it immediately — not in three days. Have a bed identified. Have luggage ready. Have someone ready to drive.</li>
          <li><strong>Realistic expectations.</strong> Even a well-run intervention doesn't always result in immediate agreement. But it plants seeds — and sometimes those seeds take root weeks or months later.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Before organizing an intervention, please call us. Our admissions team at <a href="tel:+15305282342">(530) 528-2342</a> can talk you through our process, confirm bed availability, and help you think through logistics so you're prepared.</p>

        <h2>Taking care of yourself</h2>
        <p>You cannot pour from an empty cup. The stress of loving someone in addiction is cumulative and real — it shows up as anxiety, depression, sleep problems, physical health deterioration, and isolation. You are not a bystander in this story; you are someone who is also hurting and who also deserves care.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Get your own support.</strong> Al-Anon, Nar-Anon, therapy, trusted friends — pick what works for you.</li>
          <li><strong>Separate their recovery from your worth.</strong> If they relapse, it is not your failure. Recovery is their work. You can support it but you cannot do it for them.</li>
          <li><strong>Maintain your own life.</strong> Keep going to work, seeing friends, engaging in hobbies. Shrinking your world around their problem does not help either of you.</li>
          <li><strong>Know what you can and cannot control.</strong> You can show up, be honest, hold your limits, and be present when they're ready. You cannot control the timeline.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <aside class="aside">
        <h3>Family support at Sunbright</h3>
        <div class="aside-row"><svg fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>Family involvement with resident's consent</div>
        <div class="aside-row"><svg fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>Family therapy sessions available</div>
        <div class="aside-row"><svg fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>Admissions guidance for families</div>
        <div class="aside-row"><svg fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>Intervention planning support</div>
        <div class="aside-row"><svg fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>Free SAMHSA Helpline: 1-800-662-4357</div>
        <a href="tel:+15305282342" class="btn btn-navy">Call (530) 528-2342</a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--cream">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">How Sunbright Supports Families</span>
        <h2>Healing works best when the <em>whole family is involved.</em></h2>
        <p class="lead">Addiction strains relationships. Recovery can rebuild them — but only when families are given the tools and support to heal alongside the person in treatment. Here is how we make that possible at Sunbright.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="features">
        <div class="feature">
          <div class="f-ic">
            <svg fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M17 20h5v-2a3 3 0 00-5.356-1.857M17 20H7m10 0v-2c0-.656-.126-1.283-.356-1.857M7 20H2v-2a3 3 0 015.356-1.857M7 20v-2c0-.656.126-1.283.356-1.857m0 0a5.002 5.002 0 019.288 0M15 7a3 3 0 11-6 0 3 3 0 016 0z"/></svg>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Family-Inclusive Care Model</h3>
            <p>With your loved one's consent, we keep families informed, facilitate structured communication, and create space for family sessions during treatment. You do not have to wait on the outside wondering what is happening — we make you part of the process when appropriate.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="feature">
          <div class="f-ic">
            <svg fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M8 12h.01M12 12h.01M16 12h.01M21 12c0 4.418-4.03 8-9 8a9.863 9.863 0 01-4.255-.949L3 20l1.395-3.72C3.512 15.042 3 13.574 3 12c0-4.418 4.03-8 9-8s9 3.582 9 8z"/></svg>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Family Therapy</h3>
            <p>Guided family therapy sessions address communication patterns, trust repair, and the practical questions that arise when someone comes home from residential treatment. These sessions are facilitated by a trained clinician and structured around the goal of lasting recovery — not airing grievances.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="feature">
          <div class="f-ic">
            <svg fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Aftercare Planning for the Whole System</h3>
            <p>When your loved one is ready to step down from residential treatment, we do not just hand them a discharge summary and wish them luck. We build an aftercare plan that accounts for the environment they are returning to — and we make sure you, as a family member, understand what ongoing support looks like. <a href="/programs/sober-living">Learn about sober-living options.</a></p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="feature">
          <div class="f-ic">
            <svg fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M3 5a2 2 0 012-2h3.28a1 1 0 01.948.684l1.498 4.493a1 1 0 01-.502 1.21l-2.257 1.13a11.042 11.042 0 005.516 5.516l1.13-2.257a1 1 0 011.21-.502l4.493 1.498a1 1 0 01.684.949V19a2 2 0 01-2 2h-1C9.716 21 3 14.284 3 6V5z"/></svg>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>A Direct Line for Family Questions</h3>
            <p>You can call us at <a href="tel:+15305282342">(530) 528-2342</a> or email <a href="mailto:info@sunbrightrecovery.org">info@sunbrightrecovery.org</a>. We'll answer your questions, help you understand our program, and tell you honestly whether Sunbright is the right fit for your loved one and your family's situation.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--gold">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Common Questions for Families</span>
        <h2>Answers to what families <em>most often ask us.</em></h2>
      </div>

      <div class="faq">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details>
            <summary>{f.question}</summary>
            <div class="faq-body">{f.answer}</div>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="callout" style="margin-top:32px;max-width:820px;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto">
        <svg fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg>
        <p><strong>More questions?</strong> Visit our <a href="/faqs">full FAQ page</a> for answers about program length, insurance, privacy, what to bring, and more. Or call us at <a href="tel:+15305282342">(530) 528-2342</a> — we are glad to talk.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--white">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">What We Treat</span>
        <h2>Understanding what your loved one <em>may be facing.</em></h2>
        <p class="lead">Addiction rarely looks the same from person to person — and knowing more about what drives it can help you respond with compassion rather than frustration.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <a href="/what-we-treat/alcohol" class="card is-link">
          <div class="card-tag">Condition</div>
          <h3>Alcohol Use Disorder</h3>
          <p>Alcohol is the most commonly misused substance in the US. Learn how alcohol use disorder develops, what it looks like, and how we treat it at Sunbright.</p>
          <span class="card-link">Learn more <svg fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg></span>
        </a>
        <a href="/what-we-treat/opioids" class="card is-link">
          <div class="card-tag">Condition</div>
          <h3>Opioid Addiction</h3>
          <p>Opioid addiction — including prescription painkillers and heroin — is driving significant harm in Northern California. Learn about treatment options and what recovery looks like.</p>
          <span class="card-link">Learn more <svg fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg></span>
        </a>
        <a href="/what-we-treat/dual-diagnosis" class="card is-link">
          <div class="card-tag">Condition</div>
          <h3>Dual Diagnosis</h3>
          <p>Many people with addiction also live with depression, anxiety, PTSD, or other mental health conditions. Treating both together produces far better outcomes.</p>
          <span class="card-link">Learn more <svg fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg></span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--navy section--tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">External Support Resources</span>
        <h2>You don't have to do this <em>alone.</em></h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="checks grid grid-2">
        <li><strong>Al-Anon Family Groups</strong> — Free peer support for families of people with alcohol use disorder. al-anon.org</li>
        <li><strong>Nar-Anon Family Groups</strong> — Free peer support for families of people with drug addiction. nar-anon.org</li>
        <li><strong>SAMHSA National Helpline</strong> — Free, confidential treatment referrals, 24/7: <a href="tel:+18006624357" style="color:var(--gold-lt)">1-800-662-4357</a></li>
        <li><strong>Crisis Text Line</strong> — Text HOME to 741741 for free mental health crisis support.</li>
        <li><strong>NAMI Helpline</strong> — National Alliance on Mental Illness: 1-800-950-6264 for mental health support.</li>
        <li><strong>Sunbright Recovery Center</strong> — Call us at <a href="tel:+15305282342" style="color:var(--gold-lt)">(530) 528-2342</a> or email <a href="mailto:info@sunbrightrecovery.org" style="color:var(--gold-lt)">info@sunbrightrecovery.org</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band">
    <span class="eyebrow">We're here for your family</span>
    <h2>The hardest step is the <em>first phone call.</em></h2>
    <p>Call us and tell us what's happening. You don't need all the answers before you call — that's what we're here for. Our admissions team will listen, answer your questions honestly, and help you understand whether Sunbright is the right fit.</p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <a href="tel:+15305282342" class="btn btn-gold">Call (530) 528-2342</a>
      <a href="/admissions" class="btn btn-ghost">How Admissions Works</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
